    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. The Verdict: Should You Accept, Walk Away, or Negotiate?\n\n* **Do not accept as-is:** Google almost never gives their \"best and final\" offer on the first pass. Furthermore, the base salary is lower than what you currently make, and there is no sign-on bonus.\n* **Do not walk away:** The offer is already financially superior to your current compensation in Years 1 and 2, and you have significant leverage to push it higher.\n* **Negotiate:** **Yes, absolutely.** You are a passive candidate who was recruited while happily employed at Meta. Meta IC6 to Google L6 is a lateral move between peer companies, giving you strong standing to counter.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Understanding the Math: Google's Front-Loaded Vesting\n\nGoogle does **not** vest initial new-hire RSUs (GSUs) evenly at 25% per year. Google uses a **front-loaded 33% / 33% / 22% / 12%** vesting schedule:\n\n| Component | Year 1 | Year 2 | Year 3 | Year 4 |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | $235,000 | $235,000 | $235,000 |\n| **Target Bonus (20%)** | $47,000 | $47,000 | $47,000 | $47,000 |\n| **Initial Equity ($1.08M)** | **$356,400** (33%) | **$356,400** (33%) | **$237,600** (22%) | **$129,600** (12%) |\n| **Total Comp (excluding refreshers)** | **$638,400** | **$638,400** | **$519,600** | **$411,600** |\n\n#### Key Takeaways:\n1. **Year 1 & 2 are strong:** At **~$638k/yr**, Google pays roughly **+$112k/yr more** than your current run-rate ($526k) and **+$162k more** than your next 12-month expected cash flow ($476k) at Meta.\n2. **The Year 3/4 Cliff is real:** Because initial equity drops sharply in Years 3 and 4, your base package drops to ~$520k and ~$412k. While annual refreshers (which vest evenly at 25%/year) will help offset this drop, they depend on performance and company multipliers.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Offer Evaluation\n\n* **Base Salary ($235,000):** **Slightly Below Market / Weak.** The posted range is $183k\u2013$271k. Offering $235k means they are asking you to take a $5,000 pay cut on guaranteed cash compared to Meta ($240k).\n* **Bonus ($47,000 / 20%):** Standard for Google L6.\n* **Equity ($1,080,000):** **Good initial anchor.** For Google L6 PM, initial grants typically range from $900k to $1.3M. $1.08M is around the midpoint.\n* **Sign-on Bonus ($0):** **Poor.** Google frequently utilizes sign-on bonuses ($50k\u2013$100k+) for L6 hires leaving unvested equity or cash bonuses behind at peer companies.\n\n---\n\n### 4. What Exactly to Ask For\n\nWhen countering, ask for adjustments across base, equity, and sign-on bonus:\n\n| Component | Current Offer | Your Counter Target | Rationale |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $235,000 | **$255,000 \u2013 $260,000** | You make $240k base at Meta; you should not take a base pay cut, and the posted range tops out at $271k. |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | $0 | **$75,000 \u2013 $100,000** | Offsets leaving unvested Meta equity and compensates for transition friction. |\n| **Equity (4-Yr)** | $1,080,000 | **$1,250,000 \u2013 $1,300,000** | Moves annualized RSU value from $270k/yr to ~$315k\u2013$325k/yr, softening the Year 3/4 drop. |\n\nIf Google meets you near the middle (e.g., $250k base, $1.2M equity, $60k sign-on), your **Year 1 compensation will exceed $750,000**.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Negotiation Strategy & Script\n\nBecause you are happy at Meta, communicate enthusiasm for the team while being clear that leaving your current role requires a stronger financial incentive.\n\n#### Suggested Script / Email to Recruiter:\n\n> *\"Thank you for putting this offer together. I really enjoyed meeting the team and am genuinely excited about the scope of the role and the product vision.*\n>\n> *Because I wasn't actively looking and am in a great spot with my current trajectory at Meta, compensation is a critical factor for me to make a move.*\n>\n> *Looking at the numbers:*\n> 1. *My current base salary at Meta is $240,000. I'd like to ensure I'm moving forward on guaranteed cash, so I'm looking for a base of **$255,000** (well within your posted range).*\n> 2. *Given the equity I'd be walking away from over the next year at Meta, I would need a sign-on bonus of **$80,000** to bridge the transition.*\n> 3. *To ensure competitive multi-year retention\u2014particularly given Google's front-loaded vesting structure\u2014I'd like to see the equity grant at **$1,250,000** over 4 years.*\n>\n> *If we can get the package to these terms, I would be thrilled to sign and commit right away.\"*\n\n### Next Steps:\n* Deliver the counter via phone or email, depending on your recruiter's communication preference.\n* Do not give an immediate verbal agreement if they return with an incremental increase; ask for the updated numbers in writing before making your final decision.",
