     "content": "### 1. The Verdict: **Yes, you should seriously consider accepting.**\n\nFinancially, this is an **exceptionally strong L6 Product Manager offer** for Google (top ~10th percentile for standard L6 bands) [1]. \n\nWhile they refused a cash sign-on bonus, they compensated by boosting your equity grant by **+$200,000** (from $1.08M to $1.28M) and bumping your base to match Meta ($240k). Because of Google\u2019s front-loaded vesting schedule, this gives you an enormous financial upside in Years 1 and 2 that **more than replaces the $200k of Meta equity you are walking away from**.\n\n---\n\n### 2. The Updated Numbers Breakdown\n\nGoogle new-hire grants vest **33% in Year 1, 33% in Year 2, 22% in Year 3, and 12% in Year 4**:\n\n| Component | Year 1 | Year 2 | Year 3 | Year 4 |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $240,000 | $240,000 | $240,000 |\n| **Target Bonus (20%)** | $48,000 | $48,000 | $48,000 | $48,000 |\n| **Equity ($1.28M Grant)** | **$422,400** (33%) | **$422,400** (33%) | **$281,600** (22%) | **$153,600** (12%) |\n| **Total Comp (excluding refreshers)** | **$710,400** | **$710,400** | **$569,600** | **$441,600** |\n\n---\n\n### 3. How It Compares to Staying at Meta\n\n#### Next 12 Months:\n* **Meta (Expected):** $240k (Base) + $36k (Bonus) + $200k (Vesting RSU) = **$476,000**\n* **Google (Year 1):** $240k (Base) + $48k (Bonus) + $422.4k (Vesting GSU) = **$710,400**\n* **Net Difference:** **+$234,400 (+49% increase)**\n\n> **The \"Lost Equity\" Reality:** You mentioned walking away from $200,000 in vesting Meta stock. In Year 1 at Google, you will vest **$422,400 in stock alone**, generating **+$222,400 in extra equity value** in your first 12 months. This completely covers the unvested Meta RSUs and leaves you with an extra ~$34k in bonus/cash.\n\n#### Years 1 & 2 Combined:\n* Over your first 24 months, Google will pay you **~$1.42M**. \n* At Meta's steady-state (~$526k/yr), you would make **~$1.05M**. \n* You are locking in an extra **~$370,000 in total comp** across the next two years.\n\n---\n\n### 4. What to Keep in Mind (The Caveats)\n\n1. **The Sign-On Bonus Pushback:** Google recruiters often face strict budget caps on cash sign-on bonuses for lateral transfers between FAANG/MAMAA peers unless you have a competing active offer in hand. Instead, they used equity exception approval to move your grant to $1.28M.\n2. **Year 3 & 4 Compensation Sustainability:** \n   * By Year 3, your base grant drops to $281.6k, and Year 4 drops to $153.6k.\n   * To keep your comp above $600k+ in Year 3 and 4, you will rely on **annual performance refreshers** (which vest evenly at 25%/year over 4 years) or potential promotion to L7 (Director/Principal PM).\n3. **Culture and Velocity Differences:**\n   * **Meta IC6:** Higher execution tempo, top-down project allocation, intense biannual PSC performance cycles, leaner PM-to-Eng ratios.\n   * **Google L6:** More consensus-driven stakeholder management, lower product launch velocity, but generally better work-life balance and a more predictable annual review process (GRAD).\n\n---\n\n### 5. Final Recommendation\n\n* **Take the offer** if you want a massive liquidity boost over the next 24 months (~$710k/yr) [1] and are interested in Google's scope, brand diversification on your resume, and likely better work-life balance.\n* **Stay at Meta** only if you have a near-term path to Director (IC7 / M2) within the next 6\u201312 months that would dramatically reset your Meta compensation trajectory beyond $700k+.",
